      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The development setup script falls back to piping a remote installer over HTTPS through the shell when bun is missing and npm installation fails. This is the tool vendor's documented install method and the script's purpose (preparing a developer environment) is disclosed in the repository. The behavior is not malicious and the destination is the official bun domain over TLS, but executing fetched shell content without an integrity check means the script inherits trust in that endpoint; a compromise of the vendor or the connection would run code in the developer's environment. No attacker-controlled trigger, exfiltration, or persistence is present in the supplied source, and this script is not part of the extension's shipped runtime behavior for end users.",
      "layman_explanation": "The project's developer setup script installs a build tool by downloading its official installer from the tool maker's website and running it. This is a very common practice and nothing here suggests bad intent, but it does mean the script trusts that website completely. If that website were ever compromised, a developer running the script could get unwanted code run. This affects only people setting up a development environment, not people using the extension.",
